(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-cms-exam-1-master-exam-updated-form-c"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 1 Master Exam (Updated) — Form C","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 29-year-old dental hygienist develops a painful, swollen index finger with grouped vesicles on an erythematous base, preceded by burning and tingling. A colleague suggests incision and drainage. What is the correct management?","choices":["Observe without treatment or precautions","Do not incise; cover it and start oral antivirals","Incise and drain, then give antivirals","A topical antifungal and a dry hand","Incise and drain, then give antibiotics"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Incision and drainage does not treat herpes simplex virus and can delay healing. Lesions should be covered, hand hygiene used, contact with mucosa and broken skin avoided until healed, and early oral acyclovir, valacyclovir, or famciclovir may shorten the episode. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 128"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has an itchy, sharply demarcated plaque on the proximal medial thigh extending from the crural fold. The scrotum is not involved. He also has scaling between his toes. What does the scrotal sparing indicate?","choices":["It has no diagnostic significance","It indicates erythrasma","It argues against a dermatophyte","It indicates inverse psoriasis","It supports tinea cruris; candida involves the scrotum"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. In tinea cruris the scrotum is typically spared, and scrotal involvement suggests candidal intertrigo, which commonly involves the scrotum. His coexisting tinea pedis fits, since the two often occur together. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"A 7-month-old girl has a diaper rash confined to the convex surfaces of the buttocks and upper thighs, with the inguinal folds clearly spared. There are no satellite lesions, no bullae, and no purulent drainage. Her mother reports frequent loose stools over the past week. What is the most appropriate initial management?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Leaving the diaper off entirely","A topical antifungal at each change","Oral cephalexin","Frequent changes and a thick barrier paste"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The foundation of treatment is reducing exposure to moisture and irritants through frequent changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure, and superabsorbent diapers, with a thick barrier layer applied at each change. The previous layer need not be scrubbed away if it remains clean. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man is about to be started on carbamazepine for newly diagnosed epilepsy. Which human leukocyte antigen association should prompt caution before prescribing it?","choices":["Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02","Human leukocyte antigen B*58:01","Human leukocyte antigen DQ2","Human leukocyte antigen B27","Human leukocyte antigen Cw6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Human leukocyte antigen B*15:02 is the risk allele associated with carbamazepine, while human leukocyte antigen B*58:01 is associated with allopurinol. Both are listed risk factors for Stevens-Johnson syndrome. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"A 33-year-old man is diagnosed with dermatitis herpetiformis and dapsone is planned for rapid symptom control. What must be checked before the drug is started, and what must be monitored afterwards?","choices":["Check thyroid function; monitor blood count","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; monitor renal function","Check liver function; monitor for photosensitivity","No testing or monitoring needed","Check glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; monitor blood count"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase deficiency must be checked before initiating dapsone, and the complete blood count is monitored for haemolytic anaemia and methaemoglobinaemia. Dapsone at 25 to 200 mg daily gives relief within days. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 32-year-old woman with recurrent genital herpes simplex virus type 2 infection is in a relationship with an uninfected partner. Which treatment approach also reduces transmission risk?","choices":["No antiviral therapy","Topical acyclovir during outbreaks","Patient-initiated episodic therapy","Daily suppressive valacyclovir","Prophylaxis for the uninfected partner"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent genital herpes may be managed with patient-initiated episodic therapy or daily suppressive therapy, and suppressive valacyclovir lowers herpes simplex virus type 2 transmission. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 124"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man from Connecticut presents with a swollen, painful knee two years after an untreated tick bite. What is the classic manifestation of stage 3 late persistent Lyme disease?","choices":["Symmetric small-joint polyarthritis","Erythema migrans at the bite site","Cranial nerve palsies and meningitis","A rash starting on ankles and wrists","Monoarticular arthritis of a weight-bearing joint"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Stage 3 occurs months to years later, affecting skin, central nervous system, and musculoskeletal systems, with monoarticular or oligoarticular arthritis of the knee or weight-bearing joints as the classic manifestation. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 74-year-old man presents with a two-month history of intense itching followed by blistering. Examination shows numerous 1 to 3 cm tense, firm bullae on the trunk, flexural surfaces of the arms, and the axillary and inguinal folds. Rubbing the skin adjacent to a blister does not cause the epidermis to slough. There is a single small erosion in the mouth. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Dermatitis herpetiformis","Bullous pemphigoid","Erythema multiforme","Pemphigus vulgaris","Bullous impetigo"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bullous pemphigoid damages the epithelial basement membrane, producing subepithelial blistering, and classically presents after age 60 with tense 1 to 3 cm bullae on the trunk, extremity flexures, and axillary and inguinal folds. A pruritic prodrome may precede the bullae by weeks to months, and Nikolsky's sign is negative. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man presents with an itchy, scaling annular plaque on the trunk. The clinician wishes to confirm the presence of fungal elements at the bedside before starting treatment. What is the most appropriate test?","choices":["Tzanck smear","Potassium hydroxide wet preparation","Mineral oil preparation","Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain","Patch testing"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A potassium hydroxide wet preparation detects fungal elements in skin, hair, or nail samples, and it is the appropriate bedside confirmation before treating a suspected dermatophyte infection. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 39-year-old man presents with an engorged tick attached to his flank after a hike in an endemic area. What single action is emphasised first in the management of a tick-borne illness such as Lyme disease?","choices":["Administer prophylactic intravenous antibiotics to all patients","Send the tick for species identification before any treatment","Remove the tick immediately","Apply a topical corticosteroid to the bite site","Await the appearance of erythema migrans before acting"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The management sequence begins with removing the tick immediately, followed by antibiotics, which are indicated in all stages. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman has a sacral wound showing full-thickness loss of skin extending into the dermis, and a second area on the heel showing partial loss involving only the epidermis. What are these two findings called?","choices":["Fissure and excoriation, respectively","Ulceration and erosion, respectively","Scar and scale, respectively","Excoriation and lichenification, respectively","Erosion and ulceration, respectively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Ulceration is full-thickness loss of the skin extending to the dermis or deeper, whereas erosion is partial loss of the skin of only the epidermis. Depth is the entire distinction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman with cellulitis of the leg is warned that the area may look worse on the first day of antibiotics. What explains this, and when should therapy be reconsidered?","choices":["Devitalised tissue needing debridement","The antibiotic is failing; change it on day one","The patient has become allergic","Bacterial killing inflames it; review after 7 days","Bacterial killing inflames it; review at 48 hours"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis may look and feel worse during the first day because sudden destruction of pathogens releases potent enzymes that increase local inflammation. Fever usually resolves within 24 hours, and persistence beyond 48 hours should prompt a change in antimicrobial therapy. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 12-year-old girl presents with fever and headache four days after a tick bite, and a rash has now appeared. What is the clinical triad of Rocky Mountain spotted fever, and how often is it present?","choices":["Fever, arthritis, and erythema migrans","Fever, cough, and rash","Fever, headache, and rash, in nearly all","Rash, pruritus, and lymphadenopathy","Fever, headache, and rash, in about 60 percent"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The clinical triad is fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius, headache, and rash, present in only about 60 percent of patients. Fever presents in the first 3 days with rash following 2 to 4 days after fever onset. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has confirmed tungiasis of both feet after a trip abroad. What management is appropriate?","choices":["Professional extermination of the home","Excision or cryotherapy, with tetanus prophylaxis","Oral albendazole for 3 days","Antihistamines and oatmeal baths alone","Whole-body overnight permethrin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Management includes surgical excision or cryotherapy and topical agents, tetanus prophylaxis, and systemic antibiotics. Prevention centres on avoiding walking barefoot in endemic areas. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 37-year-old woman has a firm nodule on the lower leg and reports that it occasionally hurts when knocked. What is notable about the symptom profile of a dermatofibroma?","choices":["Symptomatic only if over 3 cm","Invariably painful","Always pruritic, never painful","Usually painless, but the commonest painful skin tumour","Painful only after malignant change"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Dermatofibroma is usually asymptomatic, sometimes with a history of insect bite, and when symptomatic may cause slight pruritus or pain — it is described as the most common painful skin tumour. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"The mother of a 3-year-old boy with atopic dermatitis asks how to apply the emollient she has been given. What instruction is correct?","choices":["Apply only to inflamed areas","Withhold on steroid days","Bathe in hot water first","Apply after rinsing and avoid fragranced products","Use a fragranced moisturiser"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Prevention advice is that emollient should be applied to the skin after rinsing, the patient should avoid irritants, and skin care should be fragrance-free. Clinicians should also demonstrate medication quantity and correct application. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man has a crusted genital lesion that has been present for six days. What is the preferred diagnostic test for a suspected herpes simplex virus lesion, and how should a negative result on an older lesion be interpreted?","choices":["A potassium hydroxide preparation","A Tzanck smear, which types the virus","Nucleic acid amplification; a negative old swab excludes nothing","Viral culture; a negative result excludes infection","Immunoglobulin M serology"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Swabbing a fresh vesicle, ulcer base, or crust for type-specific nucleic acid amplification testing is the preferred test. A negative swab from an older lesion does not exclude infection because shedding is intermittent, and culture is less sensitive in healing or recurrent lesions. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman has a firm, bulbous nodule at the site of an ear piercing performed two years ago. The lesion extends well beyond the original piercing site and has continued to enlarge. What distinguishes this from a hypertrophic scar?","choices":["It stays within the wound margins","It extends beyond the margins and does not regress","It developed within four weeks","It is always painless","It flattens with time"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A keloid extends beyond the wound site margins, develops slowly, continues to enlarge for months to years, and shows no regression with a tendency to recur. A hypertrophic scar develops rapidly within four weeks, remains within the wound margins, and flattens with time. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man presents with an abrupt eruption of small pustules on the thighs and buttocks. Each pustule sits on an erythematous base and is pierced by a central hair. He is afebrile with no systemic symptoms. He has type 2 diabetes and wears occlusive workout clothing. What is the most likely causative organism?","choices":["Pseudomonas aeruginosa","Streptococcus pyogenes","Candida albicans","Corynebacterium minutissimum","Staphylococcus aureus"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Bacterial folliculitis is most commonly caused by Staphylococcus aureus. His diabetes and occlusive clothing are both listed risk factors, alongside obesity, poor hygiene, heat and humidity, immunocompromise, corticosteroids, and nasal carriage. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 58-year-old Black woman has had a pigmented band in the great toenail for two years that was previously attributed to trauma. Where does the tumour usually arise, and why is recognition delayed?","choices":["In the nail bed; only fair-skinned patients","In the matrix; pain is always present","In the periungual skin; easily seen","In the matrix; it is rare and not sun-driven","In the matrix; it is over-diagnosed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Nail unit melanoma is a rare acral melanoma arising most often in the matrix. It is not clearly ultraviolet driven and may occur in any skin tone, and delayed recognition contributes to worse outcomes. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman presents with painful genital ulcers. The clinician considers alternative causes. Which differential is characteristically painless?","choices":["Chancroid","Candidiasis","Syphilis","Trauma","Herpes simplex recurrence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Syphilis lesions start as solitary raised papules that erode and are usually painless, which is what distinguishes them from the painful ulcers of herpes simplex virus and chancroid. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 121"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with vitiligo affecting more than 5 percent of her body surface area is being counselled on treatment. What is first-line, and why is one phototherapy option preferred over another?","choices":["Topical therapy alone at any extent","Psoralen with ultraviolet A preferred","Phototherapy, narrow band ultraviolet B preferred","No treatment is available","Surgical grafting for all patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Phototherapy is first line in patients with more than 5 percent body surface area involvement, and narrow band ultraviolet B is preferred over psoralen with ultraviolet A, which carries adverse effects including increased skin cancer risk. Combination of topical therapy with phototherapy is ideal. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 31-year-old man with progressive frontotemporal recession and early vertex thinning asks about treatment. He and his partner are not planning a pregnancy and he has no contraindications. Which statement about the two principal medical therapies is correct?","choices":["Finasteride's sexual effects are permanent","Neither agent alters the course","Finasteride is topical and minoxidil oral","Finasteride is oral and works best with minoxidil","Minoxidil works best at the frontal hairline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Finasteride prevents further hair loss and may provide some regrowth, and works best in combination with minoxidil. Dihydrotestosterone is the androgen chiefly responsible for the follicular miniaturisation, which is what the enzyme inhibition addresses. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 148"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman has a rapidly expanding painful ulcer with undermined violaceous borders, and repeated wound cultures are negative. What is the most accurate description of how pyoderma gangrenosum is diagnosed?","choices":["Confirmed by wound culture","Confirmed by a specific biopsy finding","Confirmed by inflammatory bowel disease","A diagnosis of exclusion","Confirmed by a pathergy test"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pyoderma gangrenosum is explicitly a diagnosis of exclusion. Infectious ulcers, which are culture negative in pyoderma gangrenosum, and vascular insufficiency ulcers assessed by ankle-brachial index must be ruled out. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 6-year-old unvaccinated child has a generalised pruritic eruption concentrated on the trunk, scalp, and face. Macules, papules, vesicles, and crusts are all present at the same time. What feature is most characteristic?","choices":["Lesions limited to the palms and soles","Lesions with honey-coloured crusting around the nose and mouth","Lesions in multiple stages of healing appearing simultaneously","Lesions confined to a single dermatome","Lesions all at the same stage of evolution"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Primary varicella-zoster virus infection causes a generalised pruritic eruption in multiple stages of healing — macules progress to papules, vesicles, and crusts, with several stages appearing simultaneously. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 84"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man is diagnosed with pityriasis rosea. The eruption began four days ago and is mildly itchy. He is otherwise well and not pregnant-exposed or immunocompromised. What is the most appropriate management?","choices":["Oral terbinafine","Oral acyclovir for all patients","Reassurance alone, no treatment for itch","Reassurance, with an antihistamine for itch","Oral prednisone over two weeks"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pityriasis rosea is self-limited and the mainstay is reassurance. Oral antihistamines or topical steroids can be used cautiously for pruritus, and phototherapy or natural sunlight may help if begun in the first week of the eruption. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman is told she has multiple actinic keratoses and asks whether that means she has skin cancer. What is the relationship between actinic keratosis and keratinocyte carcinoma?","choices":["It progresses to basal cell carcinoma","Entirely benign and unrelated","Invasive carcinoma from the outset","It progresses to melanoma","Premalignant, on a continuum with carcinoma"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Chronic ultraviolet injury produces dysplastic keratinocytic change in a field of sun-damaged skin. Actinic keratosis is premalignant and lies on a biologic continuum with keratinocyte carcinoma. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man has had hidradenitis suppurativa of both axillae for eight years despite antibiotics and repeated drainage, and asks whether anything can cure it. What offers the best chance of permanent cure?","choices":["Wide excision of the affected areas","Long-term systemic antibiotics","Topical clindamycin used indefinitely","Intralesional triamcinolone acetonide injections","Incision and drainage of each cyst as it develops"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chances of permanent cure are best with wide excision of the affected areas. Large fluctuant cysts are incised and drained, but that manages an episode rather than curing the disease. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 79"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has not been taking a medication prescribed for her three months ago and is reluctant to say why. Which barriers to treatment and medication adherence should be considered?","choices":["Patient motivation only","Cost of medications only","Cost, health literacy, and cultural or religious factors","Barriers cannot be anticipated","Dosing complexity only"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Barriers to treatment and medication adherence include the cost and affordability of medications or services, low health literacy or lack of understanding, and cultural or religious considerations. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman presents with painful grouped vesicles on an erythematous base. The clinician performs a bedside Tzanck smear, which shows cytologic changes consistent with a herpesvirus. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Bacterial culture and sensitivity","Direct immunofluorescence of perilesional skin","Accept the Tzanck smear as definitive","A potassium hydroxide preparation","Confirm with viral polymerase chain reaction"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. A Tzanck smear provides rapid evaluation of vesicular lesions for herpesvirus cytologic changes, but polymerase chain reaction is preferred for confirmation. Viral polymerase chain reaction detects the genetic material of the virus and identifies which herpesvirus is responsible. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"A 7-year-old boy and his mother, who teaches at his school, both present with honey-crusted perioral lesions. In which patient with impetigo should a culture be obtained rather than relying on clinical appearance alone?","choices":["Any patient on topical mupirocin","Any child under five","Only those who failed oral antibiotics","A healthcare worker or teacher","Any patient with honey-coloured crusting"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cultures are obtained if the patient is at high risk for methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus infection, with healthcare workers and teachers given as the examples, or if acute post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis is a concern. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 93"},{"q":"A 9-year-old presents with a fever above 39.5 degrees Celsius for three days, headache, and now a rash that began on the ankles and wrists and is spreading centrally. What is the treatment, and does his age change it?","choices":["Supportive care only","Withhold until serology returns","Amoxicillin; doxycycline is contraindicated","Intravenous ceftriaxone for 14 days","Doxycycline, used in children for this infection"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Rocky Mountain spotted fever is treated with doxycycline in adults at 100 mg every 12 hours for 5 to 10 days, the same in pregnant women, and in children at 2.2 mg per kilogram every 12 hours. The illness is life threatening if untreated. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 48-year-old woman with papulopustular rosacea has Demodex-associated disease that has not responded to topical metronidazole. What topical agent is described as superior for this situation?","choices":["A high-potency topical corticosteroid","Metronidazole 0.75 to 1 percent gel at a higher frequency","Azelaic acid 15 to 20 percent","Brimonidine gel","Ivermectin 1 percent cream"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Ivermectin 1 percent cream is described as superior to metronidazole for Demodex-associated papulopustular rosacea, reflecting the contribution of Demodex folliculorum mite overgrowth to the pathogenesis. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 2-week-old neonate develops blisters at sites of handling and diapering, and genetic testing is planned. Which subtype carries the greatest long-term risk of squamous cell carcinoma and requires the most intensive multidisciplinary surveillance?","choices":["Kindler syndrome","Epidermolysis bullosa simplex","Acquired epidermolysis bullosa","Junctional epidermolysis bullosa","Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa causes severe scarring, and the multidisciplinary team includes haematology and oncology specifically for squamous cell carcinoma surveillance. Scarring subtypes carry the malignancy risk. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with diabetes has an asymptomatic brownish patch in a skin fold noted on routine examination. Where is erythrasma most commonly found, and what is the typical symptom profile?","choices":["Inner thighs, groin, and fourth toe web; often silent","Around the nose and mouth, honey-crusted","Scalp, thighs, and trunk, with follicular pustules","Axilla, groin, and perineum, recurrent and painful","Face and lower leg, with sudden high fever"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The most common sites are the inner thighs, crural region, scrotum, and between the fourth and fifth toes, with the axilla, under the breasts, and intergluteal folds less common. It is usually asymptomatic and may be pruritic. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has confirmed toxic epidermal necrolysis with epidermal detachment of 45 percent of his body surface area. What is the mandatory disposition?","choices":["Outpatient management","Emergency department observation","A general medical ward","A burn unit or intensive care unit","A surgical ward for debridement"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Burn unit or intensive care admission is mandatory for all cases of toxic epidermal necrolysis, together with immediate discontinuation of all suspect drugs, fluid resuscitation estimated as for major burns, and non-adhesive biological dressings. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 90"},{"q":"A 16-year-old girl has multiple 1 to 2 mm firm skin-coloured papules symmetrically distributed on the lower eyelids that appeared around puberty. What is the origin of these lesions?","choices":["Benign neoplasms of apocrine glands","Proliferation of dermal capillaries","Benign neoplasms of eccrine ducts","Collections of lipid-laden macrophages","Hyperplasia of sebaceous glands around a central pore"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Syringomas are benign neoplasms of eccrine sweat ducts. They appear at puberty and are more common in females, presenting as multiple 1 to 2 mm papules and usually asymptomatic. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"A clinician has settled quickly on a diagnosis for a 50-year-old man and is now gathering data that would support it. Which suggestion for good decision-making addresses confirmation bias most directly?","choices":["Defer decisions until all results return","Apply a guideline rule early","Ask questions that would disprove your diagnosis","Rely on quick pattern recognition","Gather all data regardless of relevance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Among the suggestions for good decision-making is to ask questions to disprove rather than confirm, alongside slowing down, being aware of base rates, considering what data is truly relevant, and actively seeking alternative diagnoses. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman of Mediterranean descent presents with painful mouth erosions that began three months ago, followed by skin lesions. Examination shows flaccid bullae and widespread crusted erosions on the scalp, chest, axillae, and groin that bleed easily and are painful. Lateral pressure on normal-appearing skin causes the superficial layers to slip away. What does the biopsy most likely demonstrate?","choices":["Band-like lymphocytes in the dermis","Subepithelial blistering, no acantholysis","Acantholysis with intraepithelial blisters","Neutrophils in a row at the junction","Granular immunoglobulin A at papillary tips"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pemphigus is characterised by loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, termed acantholysis, producing intraepithelial blisters. The oral onset, flaccid easily ruptured bullae, and positive Nikolsky's sign all identify pemphigus, and biopsy demonstrating acantholysis is the diagnostic finding. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 132"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with tinea corporis asks whether the infection can spread to her mouth, and a student asks why dermatophytes never involve mucous membranes. What is the correct explanation?","choices":["They require sebum","Salivary enzymes kill them","They can involve mucosa if immunocompromised","They are obligate intracellular organisms","They survive only on dead keratin"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Dermatophytes infect and survive only on dead keratin, involving the superficial portion of the skin at the stratum corneum, plus hair and nails. They cannot survive on mucous membranes. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"A 32-year-old fair-skinned woman has numerous moles under 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour. One lesion is very dark brown to black. What is the significance of that lesion?","choices":["It is suspicious and warrants evaluation","It confirms melanoma without biopsy","It is expected with many moles; no action","It is a Spitz nevus; always benign","It is a blue nevus; no evaluation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Common acquired melanocytic naevi are usually less than 6 mm with homogeneous surface and colour, round to oval with sharp demarcation. A very dark brown or black lesion on a light-skinned individual is specifically flagged as suspicious. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 21-year-old man presents with sudden pain and swelling over the sacrum in the gluteal cleft. Examination shows a warm, tender, erythematous fluctuant mass with purulent drainage. What is the appropriate management of this acute presentation?","choices":["Magnetic resonance imaging before any intervention","Incision and drainage","Warm compresses and observation","Immediate wide surgical excision of the natal cleft","Oral antibiotics alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An acute pilonidal abscess requires incision and drainage. The condition arises when a dimple over the coccyx draws in hair and debris leading to follicular plugging, and ingrown hairs prevent drainage and promote abscess formation. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has a biopsy-proven keratoacanthoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the standard of care, and what margin is used for elliptical excision?","choices":["Observation alone","Excision with 5 mm margins","Excision with 1 mm margins","Intralesional corticosteroid","Cryotherapy without histology"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The standard of care is to excise or destroy the tumour, preferred because of possible malignancy, with elliptical excision using 5 mm margins. Mohs surgery may be indicated for large or recurrent lesions or those in cosmetically sensitive locations. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has a biopsy-proven invasive cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma on the dorsal forearm. What is the preferred management?","choices":["Topical imiquimod or fluorouracil","Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery","Radiotherapy","Cryotherapy","Observation alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Surgical excision or Mohs micrographic surgery is preferred for invasive squamous cell carcinoma, with at least annual skin and lymph node examination and closer intervals for high-risk or immunosuppressed patients. All suspected invasive lesions are referred to dermatology or an experienced surgeon. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Computed tomography of a 48-year-old man with suspected necrotizing fasciitis shows gas in the soft tissue fascial planes. What does this indicate about the likely organism?","choices":["Gas suggests Clostridium, not group A streptococcus","Gas is present in every case","Gas suggests group A streptococcus, not Clostridium","Gas means the infection is superficial","Gas means a fungal cause"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gas may be present with Clostridium perfringens infection but is not present with group A streptococcal infection. Ultrasound is also useful for demonstrating air bubbles in soft tissue. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman has a blue-grey papule under 1 cm on the dorsum of the hand. What are blue naevi composed of, and how does management differ by lesion size?","choices":["Keratinocytes; cryotherapy","Dermal melanocytes; biopsy larger lesions","Epidermal melanocytes; always clinical","Lipid-laden macrophages; lipid screening","Dilated capillaries; blanches"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Blue naevi are composed of deeply pigmented spindle or epithelioid melanocytes in the dermis. Diagnosis is clinical for small lesions and by biopsy for larger ones, with observation and biopsy or excision if changes are noted. — CMS I Pigmented Skin Lesions - Shahsv-2.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"A 19-year-old woman is to begin oral isotretinoin for recalcitrant nodular acne. What safety requirements apply?","choices":["Monthly liver tests, no pregnancy testing","Pregnancy tests before, monthly, and 5 weeks after","No specific requirements","Pregnancy tests before and one week after","A pregnancy test before starting only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Isotretinoin is a potent teratogen. Negative pregnancy tests are mandatory prior to initiation, monthly, and 5 weeks post treatment, only one month is dispensed at a time, and providers must be registered. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 6-year-old boy has eight light brown macules greater than 5 mm and freckling in both axillae. What condition does this suggest, and what is the freckling called?","choices":["Neurofibromatosis type 1; Darier sign","Neurofibromatosis type 2","Postinflammatory hyperpigmentation","Multiple lentigines from sun exposure","Neurofibromatosis type 1; Crowe's sign"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. Café au lait spots are often the first manifestation of neurofibromatosis type 1, defined as light tan to brown macules over 5 mm prepubertally. Intertriginous freckling in the axillary and inguinal regions is Crowe's sign, with freckles smaller than 5 mm. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman has grouped papules and papulopustules around the mouth, and the clinician is distinguishing perioral dermatitis from its principal differential diagnoses. What finding would most specifically point to acne vulgaris instead?","choices":["Open or closed comedones","Central erythema with telangiectasia","Papulopustules on the face","Sparing beside the vermilion border","Scale on scalp and nasolabial folds"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Acne vulgaris is more likely when open or closed comedones are present. Perioral dermatitis produces grouped monomorphic papules, papulovesicles, or papulopustules without comedones. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 70-year-old man has a painful vesicular eruption in a band across one side of the chest. What is the single most characteristic distributional feature of herpes zoster?","choices":["It follows a Christmas tree pattern","It crosses the midline symmetrically","It is generalised over trunk and face","It stops abruptly at the midline","It is confined to skin folds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blisters are confined to the cutaneous distribution of one or two adjacent sensory nerves and abruptly stop at the midline. Thoracic dermatomes are most frequently involved at about 55 percent, followed by cranial at about 20 percent. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has soft yellow plaques on the medial aspects of both upper eyelids. What do they contain, and what should be done?","choices":["Lipid-laden macrophages; screen for hyperlipidaemia","Eccrine duct neoplasms of puberty","Lipid-laden macrophages; no investigation needed","Keratin in an epithelial cyst; excise","Dilated capillaries; laser therapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Xanthelasma consists of soft yellow cholesterol plaques formed by collections of lipid-laden macrophages. They are associated with lipid disorders, so screening for hyperlipidaemia is indicated, and they may signify increased cardiac risk. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has recurrent grouped bites appearing each morning after sleeping in her own bed. What is essential in the management of a bedbug infestation beyond treating the patient's skin?","choices":["A professional exterminator","Washing bedding at 60 degrees Celsius alone","A pediculicide to the skin","No environmental action","Treating all contacts with permethrin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Management of the patient is symptomatic with local wound care, topical antiseptic or antibiotic cream for secondary infection, and topical corticosteroids or oral antihistamines for pruritus. A professional exterminator is necessary to eradicate the infestation itself. — CMS I Dermatological Infestations - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 52-year-old man is diagnosed with a melanoma on the upper back that had spread radially for months before entering a vertical growth phase. What is the most common subtype of melanoma?","choices":["Superficial spreading melanoma","Nodular melanoma","Lentigo maligna melanoma","Acral lentiginous melanoma","Amelanotic melanoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Superficial spreading melanoma accounts for about two-thirds of cases, arises on intermittently sun-exposed skin, and evolves radially before entering vertical growth. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"A student uses the VINDICATE mnemonic to build a differential for a 72-year-old man brought to the emergency department with confusion. What do the letters stand for?","choices":["Vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, endocrine","Vascular, inflammatory, neurological, drug-related, idiopathic, cardiac, allergic, toxic, environmental","A physical examination sequence","A checklist for appraising evidence","A pretest probability score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. VINDICATE is a category-based framework: vascular, infectious, neoplastic, degenerative, iatrogenic, congenital, autoimmune, trauma, and endocrine or metabolic. Applied to confusion it yields stroke, meningitis, brain tumour, Alzheimer disease, and so on. — 1. svClinical Reasoning and Problem Solving.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"A 43-year-old factory worker splashes a concentrated alkali solution on the forearm, while a colleague of the same age has had weeks of soreness from repeated soap exposure. How does the timing of an irritant reaction to a strong alkali differ from that of a reaction to a mild irritant such as soap?","choices":["Alkalis over weeks; soaps in minutes","Both act only after weeks","Alkalis act in minutes to a day; soaps over weeks","Neither acts before 48 to 72 hours","Alkalis act systemically; soaps locally"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Symptoms present differently depending on the exposure: mild irritants such as soaps may present with subacute symptoms that progress over weeks, while alkalis and acids may present minutes after exposure or delayed up to 24 hours or more. Concentrated agents can lead to chemical burns and necrosis. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has a small area of cellulitis of the forearm with minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no comorbidity. In which patient with cellulitis is no diagnostic workup required?","choices":["Limited area, minimal pain, no systemic signs","Any cellulitis of the lower leg","Only patients under 40","Any patient who is afebrile","Any patient already on antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Cellulitis is usually a clinical diagnosis, and no workup is needed when there is a limited area of involvement, minimal pain, no systemic signs, and no risk factor for serious illness such as extremes of age or immunocompromise. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"A 44-year-old fair-skinned woman newly diagnosed with rosacea asks which trigger is most important to control. What is the most accurate answer?","choices":["Alcohol, described as the most universal trigger","Dairy products, which drive Demodex overgrowth","Topical corticosteroid use, which is the primary cause","Sun exposure, described as the most universal trigger","Cold weather, which is the dominant environmental trigger"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sun exposure is named as the most universal trigger of rosacea, alongside heat, alcohol, spicy foods, and stress. Chronic ultraviolet exposure is also a listed risk factor. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Four women in their twenties present on the same clinic list with acne of differing severity and history. For which patient is a combined oral contraceptive most appropriate as acne therapy?","choices":["A man with papulopustular acne","Mild comedonal acne, never treated","A woman planning pregnancy soon","Hyperandrogenism, unresponsive to topical therapy","Any adolescent, as first-line therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Oral contraceptives are considered for patients with hyperandrogenism and when acne is unresponsive to both topical retinoids and topical or oral antibiotics. Oestrogen decreases sebum production and reduces ovarian androgen production. — 4.  Cutaneous Bacterial Infections.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"A 29-year-old fair-skinned woman asks how to use sunscreen correctly before a beach holiday. What advice matches the guidance given?","choices":["Only for Fitzpatrick types I to III","Broad-spectrum factor 30 daily, reapplied every 2 hours","Broad-spectrum factor 30 daily, applied once","Factor 15, reapplied only after swimming","Physical barriers alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Broad-spectrum sun protection factor 30 or above is advised for daily use and 50 or above for prolonged outdoor exposure, applied 15 minutes before sun exposure and reapplied every 2 hours. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 123"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man presents with a single intensely itchy patch on the posterior neck that has been present for over a year. He describes rubbing it frequently, particularly when stressed or trying to fall asleep. Examination shows a well-demarcated thickened plaque with exaggerated skin markings, excoriation, scale, and altered pigmentation. What is the central principle of management?","choices":["Reassurance alone","Break the itch-scratch cycle","Surgical excision of the plaque","Indefinite potent topical corticosteroid","An oral antifungal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Lichen simplex chronicus results from repeated rubbing and scratching, so management centres on breaking the itch-scratch cycle with education, treatment of the trigger, emollients, behavioural substitution, nail care, and safe physical occlusion, alongside a limited course of an appropriate potent topical corticosteroid. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 183"},{"q":"A 78-year-old fair-skinned farmer has a small red, conical, hard nodule on the pinna that has ulcerated and grown over two months. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Basal cell carcinoma","Actinic keratosis","Amelanotic melanoma","Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma","Keratoacanthoma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma is the second most common form of skin cancer, usually following prolonged cumulative sun exposure, and may arise from an actinic keratosis. The classic presentation is a small red conical hard nodule that may ulcerate, or a non-healing ulcer, warty nodule, or irregular pink plaque with haemorrhagic crust. — Premalignant and Malignant Cutaneous Lesions - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has several soft whitish-yellow papules 3 to 5 mm on the forehead, each with a central dell. He is worried about skin cancer. What test can distinguish this from the main differential?","choices":["Dermoscopy","A Wood's lamp examination","Serum lipid measurement","A potassium hydroxide preparation","No test; excise every lesion"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Basal cell carcinoma is the principal differential for sebaceous hyperplasia, and dermoscopy can distinguish between them. Biopsy is performed if malignancy concern persists. — 7. Benign Skin Lesions Prof Griffenkranz 8-25-2025-2.pptx, Slide 111"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has tinea corporis, and the clinician wants an antifungal that works by destroying the fungal cell membrane rather than by blocking ergosterol synthesis. Which agent belongs to that class, and how is the class recognised by name?","choices":["Terbinafine, an imidazole, recognisable by the ending -azole","Nystatin, a polyene that treats both Candida and dermatophytes","Ketoconazole, an imidazole, recognisable by the ending -fine","Griseofulvin, an allylamine favoured for Trichophyton infection","Terbinafine, an allylamine, recognisable by the ending -fine"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The allylamine class works by destroying the cell membranes of fungi, preventing growth and ultimately killing them, and its members end in -fine: terbinafine and naftifine. Imidazoles instead block synthesis of ergosterol, a vital component of the fungal cell membrane. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman and her 62-year-old husband, who takes an immunosuppressant, both ask about shingles vaccination. Who should receive the recombinant zoster vaccine, and at what interval?","choices":["Two doses in childhood","A single dose from age 50","Two doses from age 50, or 19 if immunosuppressed","Two doses from age 65 only","Two doses only after an episode of zoster"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Two doses of recombinant zoster vaccine are given to immunocompetent adults aged 50 years and over, and two doses to adults aged 19 and over who are or will be immunodeficient or immunosuppressed, with a standard interval of 2 to 6 months. — 6. Fungal and Viral Skin Infections - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 110"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman with deeply pigmented skin asks whether she needs sun protection at all. What does the Fitzpatrick skin type classification predict, and how should it guide counselling?","choices":["The likelihood of rosacea","Baseline ultraviolet sensitivity; all types need care","The severity of an existing sunburn","The required sun protection factor alone","Baseline sensitivity; types V and VI need none"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fitzpatrick skin type predicts baseline ultraviolet sensitivity and guides photoprotection counselling. All types are susceptible to cumulative ultraviolet damage, photoaging, and skin cancer, though absolute risk varies. — 3. Dermatology  II.pptx, Slide 122"}]};
